homelab-backup-client (0.4.4) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: the same class of bug just found in homelab-backup-server's
    config editor exists here too -- `setup` on a host migrated forward
    from the pre-redesign package (whose config.yml never had a
    homelab_cli: block) would crash the first time it tried to set
    homelab_cli.api_url/email, since configedit.py could only insert a
    key under an EXISTING parent section, not create a missing one. Now
    appends a brand-new top-level section when needed, matching the fix
    on the server side.
  * Reworded "control-plane" jargon in install.sh's prompts/log lines
    toward plain language ("homelab-api login email for this host",
    "logging in to homelab-api as X"). The login-failure warning now
    also explains that (unlike the backup server's own `setup`) this
    client never creates the account -- it must already exist.

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Sun, 30 Aug 2026 11:15:00 -0500

homelab-backup-client (0.4.3) unstable; urgency=medium

  * `list-archives --archive NAME` now lists that one archive's file
    contents (`borg list <repo>::<archive>`) instead of requiring
    `restore --dry-run` with a throwaway `--target` just to browse an
    archive. `--names-only` prints bare archive names, one per line, for
    scripting -- and is what bash completion now uses: `--archive <Tab>`
    on both `list-archives` and `restore` offers real archive names,
    reusing whatever --host/--server/--ssh-user/--location/--ssh-key/
    --passphrase-file overrides are already on the command line (so a
    disaster-recovery restore against another host's repo completes
    against that repo, not this host's own). Runs via `sudo -n` (the
    config/SSH key are root-owned; a completion callback itself always
    runs unprivileged) and with stdin redirected from /dev/null (so an
    unresolvable passphrase fails fast instead of silently blocking on
    an interactive prompt during a Tab press); any failure at all --
    unreachable server, no passwordless sudo, nothing backed up yet --
    just yields no completions, same as e.g. kubectl's dynamic
    completion.
  * Fix: `ssh` had no connect timeout of its own, so an unreachable or
    misconfigured backup server address left it blocked in a raw TCP
    connect() indefinitely -- caught live via the completion work above,
    where it left orphaned `borg`/`ssh` processes running forever
    against a bad address (`sudo` starts a new session for the command
    it execs, which took them out of the process group an external
    `timeout` was trying to signal). `BORG_RSH` now always sets
    `-o ConnectTimeout=5`, matching the timeout this package's own SSH
    trust probe already uses elsewhere. This bounds every borg operation
    against an unreachable server, not just the new completion path.

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Sun, 30 Aug 2026 10:35:00 -0500

homelab-backup-client (0.4.2) unstable; urgency=medium

  * A Ctrl-C during `list-archives`/`restore`/`backup` (most commonly
    mid-restore, while borg is streaming a long file list) surfaced as a
    raw Python traceback from inside subprocess.run()'s wait loop --
    harmless, but looked like a crash. Now caught at the top level and
    printed as a plain "interrupted" message, exiting 130 (the usual
    shell convention for "killed by SIGINT").

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Sun, 30 Aug 2026 10:05:00 -0500

homelab-backup-client (0.4.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: re-running `setup`/`configure` unconditionally rewrote and
    re-enabled the backup and integrity-check systemd timers even when
    nothing about the schedule actually changed. Both timers are
    Persistent=true, so any restart/re-enable can make systemd fire an
    immediate unscheduled "catch-up" run -- observed in practice as a
    backup landing seconds after an otherwise no-op `setup` re-run, with
    a spurious "config file not found" in its log even though the config
    file was present the whole time. Two fixes:
    - install.sh now writes generated unit files atomically (temp file +
      rename) and diffs the generated content against what's already
      installed, only touching systemd (disable/rewrite/daemon-reload/
      enable) when something genuinely changed. A same-answers re-run of
      `setup` now leaves the timers' last-trigger/next-elapse state and
      unit files completely untouched. When a real schedule change does
      require a reinstall, a new log line names the Persistent=true
      timer and notes that a one-off catch-up run may follow.
    - load_config() no longer uses os.path.exists() to decide "not
      found" -- that call swallows any OSError (including a transient
      permission/IO error) into a bare False, so a real read failure and
      a genuinely-missing file produced an identical, misleading "config
      file not found" message. Now distinguishes FileNotFoundError from
      other OSErrors with separate log messages.

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Sun, 30 Aug 2026 09:45:00 -0500

homelab-backup-client (0.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * BREAKING: renamed from homelab-borg-service-backup-client to
    homelab-backup-client (binary, /etc/homelab/backup/client/ config
    path, systemd units, bash completion, man page) -- part of a wider
    redesign that replaces this package's identity/enrollment/bookkeeping
    layer with homelab-api/homelab-cli integration, while leaving all
    borg mechanics (the four backup modes, retention, restore, schedule
    jitter, key escrow) untouched. Highlights:
    - Enrollment is no longer a manual `enroll <id> <pubkey>` command run
      by an administrator on the backup server. `setup` now submits this
      host's pubkey via `homelab-cli backup enroll` once; a new
      reconciliation job on homelab-backup-server applies it
      automatically on its own timer. No SSH/sudo access to the backup
      server is or ever was needed from this side.
    - The optional run-history database feature no longer writes
      directly to Postgres with its own credentials (all psycopg2 code,
      database.* config, and install/schema.sql are gone). Each
      backup/check run is now reported via `homelab-cli backup
      report-run`/`report-check`, going through the same homelab-api
      identity layer every other homelab-* package uses. Still
      best-effort: a control-plane hiccup only logs a warning, never
      fails or blocks an actual backup.
    - backup_server/ssh_backup_server_username/backup_server_location
      (renamed from borgbackup_server/etc.) are now all optional --
      left blank, they're auto-discovered via `homelab-cli backup
      server-info`. Set backup_server explicitly to keep working through
      a homelab-api outage, or set homelab_cli.disabled: true to opt this
      host out of the control plane entirely.
    - New Depends: homelab-cli. Dropped Depends: postgresql-client,
      Recommends: python3-psycopg2 -- this package never touches
      Postgres directly, in any form, anymore.
    - A host still configured under the old
      /etc/homelab/borgbackup/ path is migrated forward automatically
      (config, SSH keypair, key-escrow -- never regenerated) the first
      time `setup`/install.sh runs on this version; the old copy is left
      in place, not deleted.
    - Reframed as a general homelab-recovery backup tool rather than
      narrowly disaster recovery for /etc/homelab/* -- nothing about the
      four backup modes themselves changed, just the README's framing.

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Sat, 29 Aug 2026 12:00:00 -0500

homelab-borg-service-backup-client (0.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * homelab_only mode no longer hardcodes per-package detection (which
    triggered a `pg_dump mailserver` default off homelab-api/processor
    merely being *installed*, and that pg_dump had no host/user at all,
    so it always failed with `role "root" does not exist` running under
    the systemd unit's `User=root`). populate_homelab_defaults() now
    globs every installed package's own
    /usr/share/homelab-<pkg>/backup-paths.txt for config paths to
    include, and dump_databases() runs every
    /usr/share/homelab-*/backup-hook.sh found (today, only
    homelab-database's pg_dumpall) -- no hardcoded per-package knowledge
    left in this tool at all; any future homelab-* package participates
    just by shipping these files. The existing manual
    homelab_only.paths/databases: config fields are untouched, purely
    additive to this.

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Sun, 23 Aug 2026 18:00:00 -0500

homelab-borg-service-backup-client (0.2.9) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: `apt remove` left a stale "Active: failed" status visible in
    `systemctl status` for units that had already failed before
    removal, even though the unit files themselves were correctly gone
    (`Loaded: not-found`) -- caught live. `systemctl daemon-reload`
    only re-reads unit files; it doesn't clear a unit's cached failed
    job state. postrm and install/uninstall.sh now also call
    `systemctl reset-failed` for each of the four units they manage.

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Sun, 23 Aug 2026 17:45:00 -0500

homelab-borg-service-backup-client (0.2.8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Default the Postgres host prompt to localhost, the pgbouncer/manual
    database host prompts to whatever Postgres host was already given,
    and the database role name (both the automatic bootstrap flow and
    the manual fallback's "Database user" prompt) to this package's own
    name -- all previously required typing the same values every time.
  * Fix: when the pgbouncer host resolves to this same host but
    homelab-pgbouncer isn't installed here, the automatic setup flow
    fell through to SSHing to itself to re-check -- caught live: this
    silently fails (no passwordless root-to-root loopback SSH set up),
    producing a confusing "pgbouncer registration unavailable/failed"
    warning instead of a clear "not installed here" outcome. Both
    run_bootstrap_app_role() and run_bootstrap_pgbouncer_entry() now
    skip the SSH fallback entirely when the target is this host, since
    SSHing to itself for a file already confirmed missing locally can't
    ever find it.

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Sun, 23 Aug 2026 17:30:00 -0500

homelab-borg-service-backup-client (0.2.7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: the automatic bootstrap flow's final verification step reused
    bootstrap_database(), which checks for the database's existence via
    a connection to Postgres's "postgres" maintenance database -- caught
    live: this fails through pgbouncer, whose [databases] stanza is a
    deliberately curated allowlist exposing only "homelab", not
    "postgres". Replaced with a direct SELECT 1 against "homelab"
    (whose existence is already guaranteed by homelab-database at this
    point) to verify the pgbouncer-fronted connection actually works.

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Sun, 23 Aug 2026 16:00:00 -0500

homelab-borg-service-backup-client (0.2.6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Phase 3 pilot of the cross-package Postgres role strategy: the
    run-tracking database prompt now offers automatic role/schema
    bootstrap via homelab-database and registration via
    homelab-pgbouncer (local or over SSH to whichever host has them),
    instead of only accepting a pre-existing set of credentials. The
    running service always connects through pgbouncer for runtime
    traffic; direct Postgres access is only ever used for the one-time
    setup step. Falls back to the previous manual host/port/user/
    password entry unchanged if neither package is reachable.

 -- Gardner <gardner@homelab>  Sun, 23 Aug 2026 15:30:00 -0500

